  data:  # fe36
    kind: work_item  # fe36
    title: "kotlin and javascript emit NO call edge for an external instance-method call, making 96% / 35% of their sink catalogues unreachable (both chained and two-step)"  # fe36
    status: todo_hard  # fe36
    priority: 1  # fe36
    parent:  # fe36
    tags:  # fe36
    - adr_0017  # fe36
    - taint  # fe36
    - analysis_quality  # fe36
    isbefore: []  # fe36
    duplicate_of: []  # fe36
    not_duplicate_of: []  # fe36
    pr_ref:  # fe36
    description: "MEASURED 2026-08-05 while re-scoping WI-makij. Sibling of that item, not a\nduplicate: WI-makij is about CHAINING and is python-specific; this is about\ninstance-method calls on an EXTERNAL type, and it swallows the two-step form\ntoo.\n\nTHE MEASUREMENT. A fixture carrying both shapes per language, so the chained\ncase has an in-fixture control, analysed by the real pipeline (hypergumbo\nsurvey), then every emitted edge run through production's own\n_match_propagation_entry against the real catalogue. Shapes chosen to mirror\nsinks the catalogue actually declares (java.io.File.writeText,\nnet.Socket.write), not invented ones.\n\n    lang         two-step method call      chained method call\n    java         emits + matches           emits + matches\n    scala        emits + matches           emits + matches\n    swift        emits + matches           emits + matches\n    python       emits + matches           NO EDGE          <- WI-makij\n    kotlin       NO EDGE                   NO EDGE          <- this item\n    javascript   NO EDGE                   NO EDGE          <- this item\n\nPOSITIVE CONTROL (L9), so the nulls are evidence rather than a broken harness:\nin the same javascript fixture, a MODULE-qualified call\n(`fs.createWriteStream(p)`) emits a calls edge AND matches the catalogued sink\n`fs.createWriteStream`. The pipeline works end to end for that shape in the same\nrun that reports nothing for `new net.Socket().write(d)`.\n\nFor kotlin, `File(p)` emits a calls edge to the CONSTRUCTOR while `.writeText(s)`\nemits nothing, in both forms — so the analyzer is reaching the expression and\ndeclining to emit for the method.\n\nEXPOSURE, from the live catalogue (post-#209):\n    kotlin       89 of 93 sinks are method-kind  = 96%\n    javascript   29 of 83 sinks are method-kind  = 35%\nSo essentially the whole kotlin sink catalogue, and a third of javascript's, is\nunreachable through the shape real code uses. Java (97% method-kind), scala\n(97%) and swift (88%) are FINE, which is why this is a per-analyzer defect and\nnot a design limit.\n\nMECHANISM, read in code for kotlin and NOT yet confirmed for javascript.\nkotlin.py's call_expression branch handles navigation_expression and resolves\nthe method through `resolver.lookup(...)`, emitting only when\n`lookup_result.found`. A JDK method is not in the repo's symbol registry, so the\nlookup fails and no edge is emitted. That also explains why the kotlin\nanalyzer's own tests pass: they assert calls to IN-REPO functions (`configure`,\n`sumSafe`), which resolve. The taint catalogue is entirely external, so it sits\nin exactly the population the analyzer drops. The javascript mechanism is\nUNMEASURED — its module-qualified path clearly works, so the two paths differ,\nbut which branch declines has not been read.\n\nUNMEASURED, DELIBERATELY: objc (56 of 62 sinks method-kind, 90%) and the other\nmethod-heavy analyzers were not fixtured. Do not assume they behave like swift\njust because swift passed — measure before scoping any fix.\n\nRELATION TO INV-labov, WHICH IS MARKED SATISFIED. Its statement is\nanalyzer-generic: \"For any method call on a local variable ... the analyzer must\nproduce a call or unresolved_external_call edge so IO boundary and taint-flow\nanalyses can detect it.\" Its root_cause names the PYTHON analyzer, its fix was\npython-only (PR #2550), its tag is `python`, and its 2026-06-08 closure cites\ntwo python tests. So the statement quantifies over analyzers while the evidence\ncovers one — L23's \"a gate's 0 violations is scoped to what its fixtures\nexecute\", at invariant scope. Flagged on that item rather than silently\nreopening it: whether the statement should be narrowed to python or the\ninvariant reopened is a scope call, and both readings leave THIS defect real.\n\nFIX SHAPE, NOT A DECISION. The obvious move for kotlin — emit an unresolved\nexternal edge when the lookup fails, which is exactly what PR #2550 did for\npython — is probably right, but its blast radius is a false-positive question,\nnot a recall one: an unresolved `.write()` on an untyped receiver is precisely\nthe INV-tapat/INV-maluk shape that io-boundary:F3's kind-aware gate exists to\nsuppress. So the edge must be emitted with the call_construct meta that lets F3\ngate it, or this trades 96% recall loss for a flood of false sinks. Measure the\nemitted volume on a kotlin repo BEFORE choosing — and note the 9-repo cohort has\nno kotlin, scala, cpp or elixir source, so a new cohort member is required to\nmeasure anything here at all."  # fe36
    fields: {}  # fe36
- op: discuss  # 71ab
